auth {
  mode: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{token}}
}

docs {
  Every request that needs a caller inherits this bearer token, so authorization is
  configured in one place. Auth > Login fills it in.

  Requests that are open to anonymous callers set their auth to none individually, rather
  than sending a token the API would ignore — sending one to POST /api/auth/2fa/verify would
  actively mislead, since that endpoint takes its challenge token in the body and rejects
  challenge tokens presented as bearer credentials.

  ## Order on an empty database

  Requests that create something store its id, and the rest of the collection reads those
  ids — so on a database with nothing in it, four requests have to run in this order before
  any other will find its subject:

    1. Auth > Login
    2. Persons > Create an administrator   (a ScopeAdmin, owning nothing yet)
    3. Scopes > Create a scope             (owned by the person from step 2)
    4. Persons > Create a User in a scope  (a member of the scope from step 3)

  The folders are ordered by subject rather than by that sequence, because a scope is the
  thing the API is organised around and reading the collection top to bottom should say so.
  Running it end to end with `bru run -r` therefore answers 404 for everything scoped until
  the four above have been run — that is the collection being a reference, not a test suite.
}
